<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reichenau Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reichenau is an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, joined to the mainland by a causeway. The Benedictine abbey founded there by the wandering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Pirmin in 724 became a major centre of learning, book painting and church building under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> and Ottonians. Its three Romanesque churches have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reichenau-island.jpg" alt="Reichenau Island" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Steiger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:InselReichenau_20090709_MartinSteiger_CCBYSA_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
